Content:
When multiplying two matrices in general, we apply the same approach as usual:

Content:
1. First, we verify that the two matrices are conformable for multiplication.
2. Then, we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second.

Content:
For example, let's find [MATH: AB,] where
[MATH: [− 2|2|1; 1|− 1|3]]
and
[MATH: [− 5|2; 2|− 2; − 4|1]]

Content:
First, we verify the conformability for multiplication:

Content:
[MATH: \underset{\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \times \color{green}{\fbox{3}}}{A} \underset{\color{green}\checkmark}{\cdot} \underset{{\color{green}\fbox{3}} \times \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{B} =
\underset{\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \times \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{C}]

Content:
So the matrices are conformable for multiplication, and the product is a [MATH: 2 \times 2] matrix. Let's set up the multiplication:

Content:
[MATH: \eqalign {
\underbrace{
\left\lbrack
\matrix {
-2 & 2 & 1 \
1 & -1 & 3
}
\right\rbrack}_{A}

\underbrace{
\left\lbrack
\matrix {
-5 & 2 \
2 & -2 \
-4 & 1
}
\right\rbrack}_{B}

&=
\underbrace{
\left\lbrack
\matrix {
\ast & \ast \
\ast & \ast
}
\right\rbrack
}_{C}
}]
Now, we calculate the entries of the product matrix (shown as [MATH: \ast]'s in the equation above).

Content:
To find [MATH: c_{11},] the entry at the intersection of the [MATH: 1]st row and [MATH: 1]st column, we calculate the dot product of the [MATH: 1]st row of [MATH: A] and the [MATH: 1]st column of [MATH: B\mathbin{:}]

Content:
[MATH: \eqalign {
\left\lbrack
\matrix {
\color{blue}-2 & \color{blue}2 & \color{blue}1 \
\color{lightgray}1 & \color{lightgray}-1 & \color{lightgray}3
}
\right\rbrack

\left\lbrack
\matrix {
\color{red}-5 & \color{lightgray}2 \
\color{red}2 & \color{lightgray}-2 \
\color{red}-4 & \color{lightgray}1
}
\right\rbrack

%	&=
% \left\lbrack
% \matrix {
% ({\color{blue}{-2}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + {\color{blue}{2}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}}) & \ast \
% \ast & \ast
% }
% \right\rbrack

= \left\lbrack
\matrix {
\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} & \ast \
\ast & \ast
}
\right\rbrack, && \quad \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} = ({\color{blue}{-2}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + {\color{blue}{2}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}})
}]

Content:
To find [MATH: c_{12},] we calculate the dot product of the [MATH: 1]st row of [MATH: A] and the [MATH: 2]nd column of [MATH: B\mathbin{:}]

Content:
[MATH: \eqalign {
\left\lbrack
\matrix {
\color{blue}-2 & \color{blue}2 & \color{blue}1 \
\color{lightgray}1 & \color{lightgray}-1 & \color{lightgray}3
}
\right\rbrack

\left\lbrack
\matrix {
\color{lightgray}-5 & \color{red}2 \
\color{lightgray}2 & \color{red}-2 \
\color{lightgray}-4 & \color{red}1
}
\right\rbrack

%	&=
% \left\lbrack
% \matrix {
% 10 & ({\color{blue}{-2}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{2}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}} \
% \ast & \ast
% }
% \right\rbrack

= \left\lbrack
\matrix {
10 & \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7} \
\ast & \ast
}
\right\rbrack, && \quad \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7} = ({\color{blue}{-2}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{2}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}}
}]

Content:
To find [MATH: c_{21},] we calculate the dot product of the [MATH: 2]nd row of [MATH: A] and the [MATH: 1]st column of [MATH: B\mathbin{:}]

Content:
[MATH: \eqalign {
\left\lbrack
\matrix {
\color{lightgray}-2 & \color{lightgray}2 & \color{lightgray}1 \
\color{blue}1 & \color{blue}-1 & \color{blue}3
}
\right\rbrack

\left\lbrack
\matrix {
\color{red}-5 & \color{lightgray}2 \
\color{red}2 & \color{lightgray}-2 \
\color{red}-4 & \color{lightgray}1
}
\right\rbrack

%	&=
% \left\lbrack
% \matrix {
% 10 & -7 \
% {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\color{blue}{-1}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{3}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}}) & \ast
% }
% \right\rbrack

= \left\lbrack
\matrix {
10 & -7 \
\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} & \ast
}
\right\rbrack, && \quad \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} = {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\color{blue}{-1}}) \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + {\color{blue}{3}} \cdot ({\color{red}{-4}})
}]

Content:
Finally, to find [MATH: c_{22},] we calculate the dot product of the [MATH: 2]nd row of [MATH: A] and the [MATH: 2]nd column of [MATH: B\mathbin{:}]

Content:
[MATH: \eqalign {
\left\lbrack
\matrix {
\color{lightgray}-2 & \color{lightgray}2 & \color{lightgray}1 \
\color{blue}1 & \color{blue}-1 & \color{blue}3
}
\right\rbrack

\left\lbrack
\matrix {
\color{lightgray}-5 & \color{red}2 \
\color{lightgray}2 & \color{red}-2 \
\color{lightgray}-4 & \color{red}1
}
\right\rbrack

%	&=
% \left\lbrack
% \matrix {
% 10 & -7 \
% -19 & {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + ({\color{blue}{-1}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{blue}{3}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}}
% }
% \right\rbrack

= \left\lbrack
\matrix {
10 & -7 \
-19 & \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7}
}
\right\rbrack, && \quad \bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7} = {\color{blue}{1}} \cdot {\color{red}{2}} + ({\color{blue}{-1}}) \cdot ({\color{red}{-2}}) + {\color{blue}{3}} \cdot {\color{red}{1}}
}]

Content:
Therefore, we have
[MATH: [10|− 7; − 19|7]]

